35. 12 PRINCIPLES OF ANIMATION

Use the 12 principles as practical engineering guidelines.

Squash and Stretch

Use subtly for:

impacts
landings
stylized attacks
fast directional changes

Do not deform characters unrealistically unless stylization calls for it.

Anticipation

Prepare the player before major movement.

Before:

punch
jump
dash
heavy attack
vault

include a readable preparatory movement.

Staging

Make important actions visually obvious.

Do not hide the important motion behind clutter.

Straight Ahead / Pose to Pose

Use authored animation for major poses.

Use procedural systems to connect and modify those poses.

Follow Through

When the main action ends, secondary body parts should continue moving.

Overlapping Action

Different body parts should not stop simultaneously.

Slow In / Slow Out

Do not move joints at constant linear speed for everything.

Arcs

Human and stylized body motion often follows curved paths.

Use curves rather than robotic straight-line motion when appropriate.

Secondary Action

Head movement, breathing, hands, shoulders, cloth, and small gestures can support the main action.

Timing

Different actions need different pacing.

Exaggeration

Increase important poses where readability benefits.

Appeal

Make poses visually interesting and character-specific.

