242. GAME FORMAT IDENTITY

The game is primarily a fighting battlegrounds experience with an RPG-scale world surrounding the combat.
Combat must remain the fastest path to mastery while exploration provides meaningful reasons to leave an arena.
The player should be able to move from open-world traversal into combat without a jarring mode switch.
Battleground encounters should support both spontaneous player conflict and structured objectives.
The world is a playground for movement, combat experiments, quests, discovery, and build expression.
Use the energy of arena fighters without reducing the RPG layer to cosmetic menus.
Use cinematic action inspiration without turning ordinary gameplay into a cutscene.
Build the rules so a player can spend an entire session fighting, exploring, looting, parkouring, questing, or customizing.
Do not force one activity on every player every minute.
The systems must reinforce one another instead of becoming disconnected minigames.
Use the existing framework and shared services whenever this feature overlaps an existing subsystem.
Prefer data-driven configuration over duplicated gameplay logic.
Keep server authority for persistent or exploit-sensitive outcomes.
Keep client responsibility focused on presentation, prediction, and responsive input.
Validate required configuration in development.
Handle missing optional configuration gracefully.
Give every temporary connection, task, cache entry, and Instance an explicit owner.
Avoid per-object loops when a central scheduler can process the same work.
Use spatial filtering before expensive operations.
Use dirty-state updates for UI and stateful presentation.
Use pooling only where repeated allocation is proven useful.
Respect quality tiers and device-specific budgets.
Support keyboard, controller, and touch whenever the feature is player-facing.
Do not allow cosmetic complexity to break gameplay readability.
Do not make a feature depend on a specific character unless that dependency is part of the design.
Expose important tuning values through configuration modules.
Keep modules focused and split them when a module becomes too large to navigate.
Avoid unnecessary OOP, metatables, inheritance, or giant stateful objects.
Use modern Roblox APIs and current task scheduling APIs.
Prefer signals and explicit state transitions over arbitrary delays.
Make failure states explicit.
Make recovery paths explicit.
Make developer diagnostics available for difficult state transitions.
Test the feature during normal gameplay, edge cases, respawns, streaming changes, and network disruption.
Check memory usage and connection cleanup after repeated use.
Check that repeated activation cannot create duplicate state.
Check that rapid input cannot bypass cooldowns or validation.
Check that the feature behaves correctly under low frame rate.
Check that the feature remains understandable without developer overlays.
Use readable naming and predictable APIs.

