393. ERROR HANDLING — PCALL-FIRST BOUNDARIES

Use protected calls broadly at meaningful runtime failure boundaries.

Prefer this development pattern when a protected boundary is appropriate:

local success, err = pcall(function()

    riskyOperation()

end)

if not success then

    error(err)

end

Always inspect the success boolean. Never treat a successful second return value as automatically being an error.

Do not discard pcall errors for required gameplay, persistence, networking, content loading, or integration operations.

pcall is for containment and diagnosis, not for hiding defects.

394. PCALL COVERAGE — RISKY OPERATIONS

Prefer protected execution around module initialization, dynamic dependency loading, content registration, asset loading, callback execution, map generation, match creation, character loading, weapon loading, powerup application, status processing, camera state transitions, physics state transitions, destruction, persistence, teleport operations where relevant, developer commands, and custom event callbacks.

Use protection around independent scheduler callbacks so one failure does not automatically terminate unrelated scheduled work.

Use protection around independently recoverable UI construction or presentation hooks when appropriate.

Do not wrap every trivial arithmetic expression, immutable table lookup, or harmless local operation in pcall merely to inflate the number of protected calls.

The target is broad protection at failure boundaries while retaining useful stack context.

395. PCALL — RE-THROW AND RECOVERY

When a protected required operation fails, capture the error, preserve diagnostics, perform only a documented safe recovery if one exists, and rethrow when the contract is violated.

Never make a broken required system appear successful just because its failure happened inside pcall.

Fallbacks must be explicit and must not erase the failure from the task ledger.

Cleanup failures should be recorded separately so the original failure remains visible.

Repeated deterministic failures must not be retried forever without a bounded policy.

396. PCALL — REMOTE HANDLERS

pcall does not provide security.

Validate remote arguments before applying gameplay effects.

Validate type, range, ownership, state, cooldown, authority, rate, and target eligibility before authoritative mutation.

Use protected execution after validation when runtime isolation is useful.

A malformed request must not crash the server and must not produce the requested reward or gameplay result.

397. PCALL — DEBUG DISCOVERY

Create consistent error messages that identify system, subsystem, operation, content ID, player or match context where relevant, and the underlying error.

Use traceable protected-call helpers when they improve stack information.

Search for ignored pcall results, empty handlers, silent fallbacks, swallowed initialization errors, and pcall wrappers that contain huge unrelated systems.

The debugging goal is to make failures easy to find, not merely to reduce visible console noise.

