Support kitchens, living rooms, bedrooms, bathrooms, offices, warehouses, shops, classrooms, lobbies, workshops, training rooms, and secret areas.

Guarantee enough clearance for combat and traversal where the space is intended for combat.

Validate required rooms for accessibility and NPC navigation.

405. MAP BUILDER — ROAD GRAPH

Build roads as a graph with nodes and segments rather than isolated decorative pieces.

Support straight segments, corners, T intersections, crossroads, roundabouts, ramps, bridges, tunnels, alleys, and service roads.

Use the graph to place buildings, crossings, street furniture, navigation anchors, events, and fast-travel points.

Validate graph connectivity after generation.

406. MAP BUILDER — PARKOUR GRAPH

Generate traversal affordance graphs across roofs, walls, ledges, vault objects, ramps, poles, beams, bridges, and other suitable surfaces.

Score routes by difficulty, distance, height, recovery options, and readability.

Create alternate routes and risk-reward shortcuts.

Never generate required traversal that can only be completed through an unreliable collision edge case.

407. MAP BUILDER — COMBAT ARENA GENERATOR

Generate arena spaces using combat-aware geometry.

Control cover, line of sight, wall access, verticality, escape paths, spawn separation, destructible density, powerup anchors, and camera clearance.

Create variants for one-on-one, team, free-for-all, and boss encounters.

Run a validation pass before allowing a generated arena into a match pool.

408. MAP BUILDER — BIOME PACKS

Create reusable biome packs such as urban, suburban, industrial, forest, mountain, wetland, river, rooftop, underground, cave, ruins, arena, construction, market, and coastal-like original regions.

Each biome owns terrain, vegetation, rocks, props, structures, audio, lighting, NPC, event, and loot rules.

Biome packs should be composable so maps can combine regions without duplicating code.

409. MAP BUILDER — TRANSITION ZONES

Blend neighboring biomes through transitional material, vegetation, rock, lighting, audio, and architecture rules.

Avoid abrupt seams unless the boundary is intentionally dramatic.

Use transition width as a configuration value.

Validate that transitions do not create unreadable traversal or accidental collision walls.

410. MAP BUILDER — LANDMARK LIBRARY

Create original landmark families including towers, plazas, collapsed structures, giant statues, rooftop monuments, transit ruins, bridge arenas, flooded chambers, observatories, greenhouses, industrial stacks, underground halls, and unusual traversal structures.

Landmarks should create navigation anchors and gameplay opportunities.

Use rarity weights so not every map receives the same landmark pattern.

411. MAP BUILDER — CHUNK STREAMING

Use chunk lifecycle states such as unloaded, queued, generating, generated, active, low-detail, unloading, and disposed.

Use ownership tokens so canceled generation can cleanly stop.

Do not fully simulate distant chunks when only a low-detail representation is needed.

Validate chunk seams and navigation at boundaries.

412. MAP BUILDER — SEED ARCHITECTURE

Use deterministic seed domains for terrain, vegetation, structures, roads, loot, NPCs, powerups, events, and decoration.

Keep seed domains independent enough that changing a cosmetic pass does not reshuffle critical objectives unnecessarily.

Persist the generator version and relevant seed information for bug reproduction.

413. MAP BUILDER — QUALITY SCORING

Calculate map quality scores for reachability, path diversity, verticality, combat space, parkour density, landmark spacing, spawn fairness, objective access, visual repetition, and estimated performance cost.

Regenerate or repair maps that fail hard thresholds.

Expose the score in developer tools.

414. MAP BUILDER — AUTHOR OVERRIDES

Allow designers to lock roads, landmarks, arenas, objectives, spawn points, boss zones, story rooms, parkour routes, and protected structures.

Procedural generation must respect locked regions.

Author overrides should survive deterministic regeneration.

415. MAP BUILDER — ACTUAL CONTENT OUTPUT

A generator does not count as completed map content by itself.

Generate representative maps from multiple seeds.

Inspect and playtest those generated outputs.

Use the generated results to discover bugs in the generator and the shared framework.

Keep sample generated maps or reproducible generation data for regression tests where practical.

416. UI — SCALE-FIRST LAYOUT

Audit every screen and major panel for responsive sizing.

Prefer UDim2 Scale for major layouts and use Offset only for intentionally pixel-like details or controlled micro-layouts.

Use UIPadding, UIListLayout, UIGridLayout, UISizeConstraint, UITextSizeConstraint, UIAspectRatioConstraint, and other appropriate layout constraints to maintain stable composition.

Do not use absolute offsets as a shortcut for centering entire interfaces.

417. UI — ANCHORPOINT CENTERING

Use AnchorPoint to define the reference point of a component.

Centered panels should typically use a centered AnchorPoint and Scale-based Position.

Left, right, top, and bottom aligned components should use anchors that match their intended reference edge.

Audit every centered UI object for unnecessary offset-based placement.

418. UI — ASPECT RATIO CONTROL

Use UIAspectRatioConstraint where distortion would damage the component.

Apply constraints selectively instead of adding aspect constraints to every Frame.

Protect character portraits, icons, buttons, meters, and other fixed-ratio content from stretching.

Validate at wide, standard, square-ish, and narrow viewports.

419. UI — SIZE CONSTRAINTS

Use minimum and maximum size limits where a component can become unusable or absurdly large.

Ensure touch controls remain usable while desktop layouts do not become comically oversized.

Use text size constraints or responsive text policies to reduce clipping.

420. UI — SAFE AREA

Create a reusable safe-area policy for mobile notches, gesture areas, platform overlays, and edge-adjacent controls.

Keep critical controls away from unsafe edges.

Allow cosmetic elements to extend farther than interactive elements when appropriate.

421. UI — RESPONSIVE BREAKPOINTS

Use viewport-aware layout tiers instead of hard-coding dozens of device model checks.

Support compact, standard, wide, ultrawide, touch, and controller-oriented compositions where needed.

Keep breakpoints configurable.

Test transitions near breakpoint boundaries.

422. UI — 3D PRESENTATION MODE

Build an optional 3D UI presentation layer using appropriate world-space or viewport-based UI techniques.

Support world-space health bars, quest markers, ability indicators, interactive hologram-like panels, 3D character cards, and weapon previews.

The same gameplay data must feed both 2D and 3D presentation modes.

Do not duplicate core gameplay state just because presentation changes.

423. UI — 3D UI SETTINGS

Provide configurable presentation choices such as 2D, 3D, Hybrid, and Minimal when the feature supports multiple modes.

Allow distance scaling, opacity, occlusion behavior, maximum visible count, and effect intensity to be configured.

Make the choice real; do not build a setting that only changes its own label.

424. UI — WORLD-SPACE PERFORMANCE

Cull distant world-space UI.

Throttle updates for distant markers.

Pool reusable world markers when profiling shows frequent allocation.

Limit expensive ViewportFrame scenes and animated 3D previews.

Use priority so important markers survive cosmetic overload.

425. UI — SCREEN-SIZE PREVIEW TOOL

Create a developer preview that cycles through representative viewport sizes.

Test compact mobile, tablet, standard desktop, ultrawide, and console-like dimensions.

Flag clipping, overlap, stretched assets, unreadable text, off-screen controls, and broken anchors.

426. UI — CUSTOM HUD PRESETS

Support Classic, Minimal, Competitive, Cinematic, Touch, Controller, and Custom HUD presets.

Presets should change layout configuration rather than duplicating entire UI trees where practical.

User-customized HUD positions should remain within safe bounds.

427. UI — SEARCHABLE SETTINGS

Create searchable settings for large configuration menus.

Support aliases for terms such as camera, shake, FOV, HUD, text, VFX, controller, touch, interface, and performance.

Keep setting IDs stable so search and saved preferences survive layout changes.

428. CAMERA — SINGLE AUTHORITY

Create one shared camera system responsible for composing camera contributions.

Do not allow random gameplay scripts to continuously overwrite Camera.CFrame.

All camera contributors should register intent through the shared system.

429. CAMERA — CONTRIBUTION STACK

Support camera layers for base follow, combat, movement, target focus, recoil, shake, finisher, ability, cinematic, spectator, and photo mode.

Each contribution can define priority, weight, duration, offset, rotation, FOV contribution, and blend behavior.

Interrupted contributions must cleanly unregister.

430. CAMERA — SHAKE SYSTEM

Create reusable camera shake impulses for impacts, dashes, landings, explosions, finishers, boss attacks, destruction, and other major events.

Expose amplitude, frequency, duration, direction, falloff, priority, and stacking policy.

Honor reduced-shake and disabled-shake settings.

431. CAMERA — FOV SYSTEM

Allow controlled FOV contributions for sprinting, dashing, falling, speed boosts, slow-motion moments, ultimates, and cinematic reveals.

Bound the total FOV change and smoothly restore the baseline.

Do not let multiple systems fight over a single absolute FOV value.

432. CAMERA — OCCLUSION

Handle walls, ceilings, props, crowded players, tight corridors, and parkour surfaces.

Prefer smooth contextual repositioning over sudden camera teleportation.

Test camera behavior near world-space UI and combat targets.

433. CAMERA — COMBAT FRAMING

Keep attackers and targets readable in one-on-one combat.

For group combat, use bounded heuristics to avoid extreme zoom or rotation oscillation.

Favor gameplay clarity over cinematic framing during normal combat.

434. CAMERA — PARKOUR FRAMING

Preserve forward route visibility during wall runs, vaults, ledge movement, dives, and rooftop traversal.

Use look-ahead and damping instead of overreacting to tiny direction changes.

Keep camera transitions consistent with movement momentum.

435. CAMERA — FINISHER HANDOFF

Build a prepare, blend, execute, restore pipeline for finishers and cinematic attacks.

Restore FOV, player control, shake settings, and target facing after interruption.

Do not leave the camera locked after death, disconnect, round end, or canceled abilities.

436. PHYSICS — OWNERSHIP MODEL

Define ownership policies for every dynamic gameplay object.

Classify objects as server-owned, client-predicted presentation, client-only cosmetic, networked dynamic, static, or pooled.

Exploit-sensitive physics results must remain authoritative.

437. PHYSICS — KNOCKBACK PROFILES

Use reusable knockback profiles with horizontal force, vertical force, direction source, falloff, air multiplier, wall interaction, and recovery behavior.

Integrate knockback with hitbox classes rather than duplicating it in every attack.

Test extreme values and simultaneous knockbacks.

438. PHYSICS — PROJECTILE FRAMEWORK

Support hitscan, linear, curved, homing, bouncing, piercing, orbiting, boomerang, chained, and trap-like projectile models.

Separate projectile motion from projectile hit behavior.

Every projectile owns cleanup, hit deduplication, lifetime, and budget state.

439. PHYSICS — RAGDOLL

Support controlled ragdoll entry, partial ragdoll where useful, recovery, cleanup, and respawn restoration.

Prevent abandoned constraints and motors from surviving character lifecycle changes.

Ragdoll should not unintentionally bypass authoritative death state.

440. PHYSICS — DEBRIS BUDGET

Classify debris as cosmetic, light-physics, networked-physics, or persistent objective debris.

Use the lowest complexity that satisfies the feature.

When overload occurs, reduce cosmetic debris first.

441. WEAPONS — DATA-DRIVEN FRAMEWORK

Build weapon definitions with identity, class, rarity, stats, resources, combo profile, ability slots, passives, status interactions, animation profile, VFX, audio, camera behavior, and cosmetic data.

Do not hard-code each weapon as a fork of the same giant script.

Use weapon archetypes plus specialized behavior modules where truly necessary.

442. WEAPONS — ORIGINAL ARCHETYPES

Support original archetypes including short blades, long blades, heavy weapons, staffs, spears, hammers, shields, gloves, gauntlets, chained tools, throwable tools, focus relics, mechanical devices, transforming weapons, and improvised weapons.

Each archetype must differ in timing, range, commitment, movement, or resource interaction rather than only damage numbers.

443. WEAPONS — TRANSFORMATION

Allow weapons to fold, split, extend, reconfigure, change grip, reveal secondary forms, create temporary attachments, or change energy state.

Transformations need authored or procedural animation, VFX, audio, camera feedback, authoritative state, and cleanup.

Keep transformation duration and cancellation rules explicit.

444. WEAPONS — MASTERY

Use weapon mastery to unlock new expression such as techniques, stances, combo routes, counters, mobility interactions, presentation variants, and training drills.

Avoid turning mastery into only repetitive stat inflation.

445. POWERUPS — ANIMATED PICKUPS

Create a generic animated powerup framework with idle motion, pulsing, orbiting accents, hover motion, light pulses, pickup anticipation, pickup burst, audio, and optional world-space text.

Use pooled or centralized update strategies for high counts.

Every powerup owns duration, stacking, expiration, and cleanup.

446. POWERUPS — UNIQUE EFFECT CATEGORIES

Support movement, jump, damage, defense, resource, cooldown, status, shield, stealth, tracking, weapon, transformation, and ultimate-related powerups.

Use explicit stacking and combination rules.

Powerups must be authoritative and resistant to fake client pickup claims.

447. POWERUPS — SPAWN DIRECTOR

Use map anchors, weighted spawn tables, event state, round phase, player density, combat intensity, and anti-camping rules.

Rare powerups can have special announcement and contest mechanics.

Do not let multiple spawn systems create uncontrolled item inflation.

448. STATUS — INTERACTION MATRIX

Centralize status application, stacking, refreshing, replacement, cleanse, immunity, resistance, conversion, amplification, suppression, and trigger behavior.

Keep status outcomes deterministic and authoritative.

Do not implement status-to-status interactions as scattered pairwise special cases.

449. CHARACTERS — PASSIVE ENGINE

Implement exactly two primary passives per playable character through shared passive hooks.

Passive triggers may include combat start, first hit, repeated hit, resource threshold, health threshold, parry, perfect block, airborne state, status events, environmental events, and match events.

Passives must change meaningful decisions instead of existing only as tiny unconditional stat boosts.

450. CHARACTERS — UNIQUE PLAYSTYLE

Every playable character must have a distinct combat rhythm, movement tendency, resource behavior, status relationship, animation language, VFX identity, audio identity, and UI presentation.

Never solve content variety by recoloring one generic moveset.

451. MATCHMAKING — MODES

Support data-driven casual, ranked, duel, team battle, free-for-all, private match, training, custom lobby, and rotating modes.

Each mode declares team size, player caps, map pool, duration, rules, reconnect behavior, spectator rules, and reward policy.

Do not create a separate hard-coded matchmaking implementation for every mode.

452. MATCHMAKING — QUEUE STATE

Support join, leave, queue heartbeat, timeout, skill range, latency/region preference, party compatibility, duplicate prevention, match formation, cancellation, and failover.

Handle simultaneous queue requests deterministically.

Never leave a player permanently stuck in a queue after a failure.

453. DUELS — FULL FLOW

Create challenge, accept, reject, expiration, arena selection, character selection, loadout lock, ready, countdown, round, result, rematch, forfeit, and exit flows.

Use authoritative match state and reward handling.

Keep the interaction fast enough that players do not spend longer waiting than fighting.

454. SPECTATOR — CAMERA AND UI

Support target cycling, camera follow, match timer, player names, status information, score, objective state, and exit behavior.

Spectator camera must use the shared camera system.

Apply information visibility rules so spectators do not receive private data unintentionally.

455. RECONNECT — SESSION RESTORATION

Where a mode supports reconnecting, restore authoritative match state, loadout, score, round, and remaining time within a bounded reconnect window.

Handle expired windows safely.

A disconnected player must not create duplicate rewards by reconnecting repeatedly.

456. PARTIES — SAFE QUEUING

Support invite, accept, leave, kick where appropriate, leader transfer, ready state, privacy, queue as party, and party-size constraints.

Protect against race conditions when members join or leave simultaneously.

Keep party presentation separate from matchmaking authority.

457. CUSTOM RULES — DATA-DRIVEN

Allow configurable match modifiers such as low gravity, high gravity, limited healing, random characters, random weapons, destructible arenas, status chaos, parkour-heavy movement, or special objective rules.

All rules must be server authoritative.

Do not allow client-side rules to alter competitive match outcomes.

458. COMBAT — FEEDBACK STACK

Layer animation response, hitstop, camera impulse, VFX, sound, damage numbers, status indicators, combo feedback, controller feedback, and target reaction in a controlled priority order.

Increase response intensity for major impacts.

Avoid feedback saturation when many players attack simultaneously.

459. COMBAT — CANCEL GRAPH

Represent attack transitions as a configurable graph supporting startup, active, recovery, hit-confirm, block-confirm, parry, aerial, wall, resource-gated, character-specific, and weapon-specific transitions.

Expose graph inspection tools for developers.

Prevent accidental infinite cancellation routes.

460. COMBAT — HITBOX CLASS SYSTEM

Continue using a shared hitbox profile system so attacks can reference Blockable, Unblockable, GuardBreak, Parryable, IFrameRespecting, IFrameIgnoring, MultiHit, OneHitPerTarget, Projectile, AoE, Grab, Launch, and other profiles.

Keep universal behavior centralized.

Only special-case genuinely unique interactions.

461. COMBAT — ENVIRONMENTAL INTERACTION

Allow attacks to use walls, pillars, breakable cover, elevation, debris, hazards, and map features where intentional.

Environmental effects must obey hitbox profiles, authority, destruction budgets, and cleanup.

Create reusable environment interaction profiles.

462. COMBAT — AIR GAME

Support launchers, air follow-ups, air dodges where appropriate, air heavies, ground spikes, wall bounce, and aerial finishers.

Use diminishing control or anti-infinite rules when needed to prevent endless air loops.

Preserve player agency and clear recovery windows.

463. MOVEMENT — INPUT RESPONSIVENESS

Use a small action buffer for movement and combat inputs.

Support buffered jump, dash, attack continuation, block, ability, and parkour transitions where appropriate.

Expire buffered actions quickly and deterministically.

Do not allow buffering to bypass cooldowns or state restrictions.

464. MOVEMENT — PROCEDURAL FEEL

Use acceleration, deceleration, momentum, nonlinear curves, cubic interpolation, Bezier trajectories, sine/cosine secondary motion, raycast or shapecast sensing, and subtle body counter-motion where useful.

Prioritize smoothness and natural transitions over robotic interpolation.

Keep authoritative movement rules distinct from cosmetic body motion.

465. MOVEMENT — STATE MACHINE

Use explicit states for idle, run, sprint, jump, fall, land, dash, slide, roll, wall-run, wall-jump, vault, mantle, attack, block, parry, stun, knockdown, ragdoll, cinematic, and dead where applicable.

State priority must be deterministic.

Every state transition must clean up temporary tasks and animation layers.

466. PARKOUR — COMBAT BRIDGING

Allow traversal to flow directly into attacks without losing facing, momentum, hitbox timing, camera framing, or animation continuity.

Provide wall-run strikes, vault attacks, slide attacks, dive attacks, wall-jump attacks, and other original traversal-combat links.

Validate transitions against collision and state rules.

467. ANIMATION — LAYER MANAGER

Use layered animation for locomotion, combat, aim, upper body, damage reaction, movement additive, face, idle secondary motion, weapon, and cinematic states.

Define priorities and blending behavior explicitly.

Do not let procedural systems permanently destroy authored animation state.

468. ANIMATION — HUMAN PROCEDURAL MOTION

Use inertia, anticipation, balance, weight transfer, shoulder lag, hip counter-rotation, head stabilization, delayed accessories, asymmetry, overshoot, and recovery.

Use controlled irregularity rather than random jitter.

Use authored poses for major actions and procedural motion for connective and secondary behavior.

469. VFX — PRIORITY AND QUALITY

Create Minimal, Low, Medium, High, and Ultra effect profiles where practical.

Prioritize major combat feedback, dangerous telegraphs, player-owned attacks, boss events, and match results.

Drop or simplify low-priority cosmetic effects under load.

Every temporary effect must have an owner, lifetime, reset, and cleanup path.

470. AUDIO — STATEFUL PRESENTATION

Create music states for exploration, combat, boss, dialogue, menu, match start, match point, victory, defeat, and world events.

Use reusable audio definitions with priority and bounded voice counts.

Prevent sound cloning storms during high-frequency combat.

471. WORLD EVENTS — DIRECTOR

Create a world event director that balances bosses, assaults, races, rescues, resource surges, destruction challenges, roaming encounters, parkour challenges, and special events.

Use regional budgets and cooldowns.

World events should be discoverable without overwhelming the normal gameplay loop.

472. NPC — LOD AND REACTIONS

Use Full, Reduced, Simplified, and Paused simulation tiers.

Allow NPCs to react to combat, destruction, weather, player reputation, bosses, and world events.

Distant NPCs should not run expensive decision logic when the player cannot meaningfully observe it.

473. BOSS — PHASE GRAPH

Use explicit phase graphs with thresholds, attacks, movement profiles, telegraphs, arena behavior, music, VFX, camera behavior, and transition cleanup.

Berserk or final-phase behavior should be readable and deliberate.

Bosses should interact with the same hitbox, status, physics, and camera frameworks as other content.

474. INVENTORY — SCALABLE DATA MODEL

Support stacking, sorting, filtering, searching, favorites, locking, equipping, comparisons, rarity, categories, cosmetics, and authoritative ownership.

Keep storage data separate from UI presentation.

Use virtualization for large collections.

475. SHOP — SERVER AUTHORITY

Validate prices, stock, ownership, currency, quantity, purchase limits, and eligibility on the server.

Client UI is a presentation layer and request source, not a pricing authority.

Make purchases idempotent where practical.

476. REWARD — TRANSACTION FLOW

Use authoritative reward transactions with unique IDs where practical.

Grant rewards independently from animation completion so a skipped reveal cannot lose the reward.

Prevent repeated requests from granting the same reward twice.

Provide developer reward audit records.

477. SAVE/LOAD — FAILURE SAFETY

Validate loaded data against versioned schemas.

Use bounded retries for recoverable persistence failures.

Never fabricate valuable ownership on load failure.

Keep transaction and migration identifiers where needed to prevent duplication.

478. DEVELOPER TOOLS — CONTENT INSPECTOR

Create tools for searching characters, weapons, abilities, statuses, powerups, maps, biomes, quests, NPCs, bosses, VFX, audio, and animations by ID, name, category, tag, owner, or status.

Display missing references and duplicate IDs.

Make content debugging faster than manually searching the Explorer.

479. DEVELOPER TOOLS — CAMERA INSPECTOR

Display active camera contributors, priorities, weights, shake impulses, FOV contributions, targets, occlusion state, and blend state.

Provide a way to freeze or inspect a single contribution in development.

480. DEVELOPER TOOLS — MAP INSPECTOR

Display biome, chunk, generator version, seed, spawn anchors, parkour routes, combat zones, event anchors, LOD, and budget information.

Allow regeneration of one isolated region without rebuilding the entire world.

481. DEVELOPER TOOLS — ERROR CATALOG

Group repeated errors by fingerprint and track first seen, last seen, count, system, subsystem, and relevant context.

Do not flood the console with identical messages while still making the failure obvious.

Provide filters for critical, gameplay, network, UI, map, VFX, and persistence errors.

482. DEVELOPER TOOLS — CONNECTION AND TASK AUDIT

Track live connections and scheduled tasks by owner.

Flag callbacks that survive expected lifecycle boundaries.

Flag high-frequency tasks and repeated failed retries.

Use the audit repeatedly during respawn, match, UI, map, and VFX stress tests.

483. PERFORMANCE — BUDGET ARCHITECTURE

Define budgets for CPU work, VFX, audio voices, physics, AI, UI instances, map generation, world-space UI, network updates, and debris.

Do not wait for a feature to become expensive before defining its budget.

Use measured bottlenecks to decide which budgets need more headroom.

484. PERFORMANCE — SCHEDULER

Use a central scheduler for repeated work when it reduces thousands of independent timers or loops.

Support one-shot, repeating, owner, cancel, pause, resume, priority, batch, and throttle behavior where useful.

Ensure the scheduler itself is profiled and does not become a bottleneck.

485. PERFORMANCE — SPATIAL FILTERING

Use spatial partitioning, region membership, relevance checks, and distance thresholds before expensive work.

Do not scan the entire workspace or every player for every small world interaction when a smaller candidate set exists.

486. PERFORMANCE — DIRTY STATE

Update presentation when meaningful state changes instead of rewriting every value every frame.

Apply dirty-state logic to HUD, inventory, markers, nameplates, world UI, and replicated cosmetic presentation.

487. PERFORMANCE — MEMORY REGRESSION

Repeat join, leave, respawn, character swap, match creation, match completion, UI open/close, VFX spawn/cleanup, and map load/unload cycles.

Compare connection counts, Instance counts, memory, and task counts before and after repeated cycles.

Treat upward trends without an intentional cache policy as a likely leak.

488. PERFORMANCE — NETWORK

Batch state updates where practical.

Send only changed or relevant information.

Use proximity or relevance filtering for cosmetic effects.

Use compact representations when high-frequency data justifies them.

Never let bandwidth reduction remove authoritative validation.

489. PERFORMANCE — VFX

Use pooling for high-churn effect objects when profiling supports it.

Use quality tiers, distance culling, event prioritization, and bounded particle counts.

Do not pool every object by default.

490. PERFORMANCE — UI

Avoid rebuilding complete menus for one changed row.

Virtualize large collections.

Limit expensive ViewportFrame previews.

Audit AutomaticSize chains and layout recalculation costs.

Destroy or deactivate hidden UI according to the architecture rather than leaving every hidden screen active forever.

491. SECURITY — CLIENT AS HOSTILE

Treat all client-supplied combat, inventory, currency, reward, movement-sensitive, status, match, and destruction data as untrusted.

Validate intent rather than accepting client-reported outcomes.

Use rate limits, state checks, ownership checks, and server authority.

Never rely on pcall, obfuscation, or hidden remotes as a security mechanism.

492. QA — FEATURE CHECKPOINTS

Continue the existing feature-level playtest batching policy.

Do not start Play mode after every tiny fix.

Batch compatible implementation and fixes, statically validate, then perform one meaningful feature-level Play test.

Only use broader Play tests at integration, progression, regression, or final gates.

493. QA — FAILURE VARIANTS

For applicable features, test rapid repetition, interruption, death, respawn, teleport, disconnect, streaming changes, network delay, invalid remote data, missing configuration, large object counts, low frame rate, device input, UI scale changes, quality changes, character swaps, and cleanup reuse.

Do not assume a passing basic test proves all variants.

494. QA — WHOLE GAME JOURNEY

Play through launch, main menu, character select, loadout, first movement, first fight, first ability, first quest, first reward, first customization, first match, victory/defeat, and return to menu.

Look for confusing transitions, stuck states, dead buttons, broken focus, camera resets, missing feedback, and progression failures.

495. ACCESSIBILITY — MOTION

Provide reduced motion, reduced camera shake, reduced screen flash, and related comfort settings where feasible.

Critical gameplay information must remain available when effects are disabled.

496. ACCESSIBILITY — INPUT

Support keyboard, mouse, controller, and touch as applicable.

Use the shared action abstraction so UI prompts update automatically when input device changes.

Ensure controller focus and touch targets remain usable at compact scales.

497. ACCESSIBILITY — COLOR AND TEXT

Never use color as the only status indicator.

Use icons, shape, text, patterns, or animation in addition to color.

Support scalable readable text and test longer text lengths.

498. CONTENT — SCALE OF ROSTER

Build the architecture so adding dozens of characters, hundreds of weapons, thousands of items, and large map catalogs remains manageable.

Prefer registries and data definitions over duplicated system logic.

Virtualize UI collections and validate registries automatically.

499. CONTENT — ORIGINALITY ENGINE

Create original characters, mechanics, names, costumes, weapons, statuses, maps, UI art, VFX, sounds, and dialogue.

Use inspiration from gameplay principles without recreating distinctive copyrighted content.

When an idea resembles a recognizable signature mechanic too closely, redesign it into an original identity before implementation.

500. CONTENT — CHARACTER UNIQUENESS MATRIX

For each character compare movement profile, attack rhythm, defensive identity, resource loop, passives, status interactions, combo structure, VFX, audio, camera, and UI presentation against the existing roster.

Flag characters that are functionally recolors of an existing moveset.

501. CONTENT — WEAPON UNIQUENESS MATRIX

Compare weapon range, timing, commitment, mobility, hitbox shapes, resource interactions, stance behavior, passives, and transformations.

Prevent a weapon catalog from becoming many copies with different skins.

502. CONTENT — MAP UNIQUENESS MATRIX

Compare routes, verticality, arena geometry, landmark placement, biome composition, traversal patterns, event anchors, and visual identity.

Procedural maps should still feel intentionally authored through controlled composition.

503. WORLD — WEATHER

Support optional clear, overcast, rain, storm, mist, and original snow-like presentation states.

Blend sky, fog, particles, audio, lighting, and environmental cues.

Gameplay changes from weather must be deliberate and configuration-driven.

504. WORLD — TIME OF DAY

Use configurable day/night cycles with optional NPC, shop, event, spawn, and ambient hooks.

Prefer low-cost environment state changes over per-part simulation.

505. WORLD — AMBIENCE DIRECTOR

Balance NPC activity, ambient audio, weather, particles, world events, wildlife, and distant VFX by region.

Prevent ambient systems from consuming the same budgets needed for combat.

506. WORLD — REGION MANAGER

Divide the world into logical regions tracking players, activity, chunks, NPC budgets, events, and world state.

Use region data for spawn, LOD, event scheduling, and performance decisions.

507. WORLD — SECRET AREAS

Generate controlled secrets using alternate entrances, environmental clues, breakable walls, parkour shortcuts, NPC rumors, anomalies, and rare events.

Keep secret content original and avoid requiring exploits for normal completion.

508. SOCIAL — PROFILE SHOWCASE

Allow players to showcase favorite character, weapon, titles, achievements, cosmetics, and selected statistics.

Keep private/moderation-sensitive information out of public showcases.

509. SOCIAL — SPARRING

Support safe player-requested sparring with configurable damage, round, and reset rules.

Sparring should not grant unintended progression or rewards unless intentionally configured.

510. SOCIAL — PLAYER BLOCKING

Provide block, mute, report, and party privacy controls where supported.

Keep moderation actions separate from cosmetic customization systems.

511. QUALITY OF LIFE — FAVORITES

Allow players to favorite characters, weapons, items, quests, maps, emotes, and cosmetics where useful.

Use favorites to improve filtering and quick selection.

512. QUALITY OF LIFE — RECENT ITEMS

Expose recently used or acquired items in menus where it reduces navigation time.

Keep recent-item storage bounded.

513. QUALITY OF LIFE — QUICK LOADOUTS

Allow multiple loadout presets for compatible modes.

Validate owned content and mode restrictions whenever a preset is loaded.

514. QUALITY OF LIFE — SEARCH EVERYWHERE

Build reusable search behavior for characters, items, quests, cosmetics, settings, maps, and content catalogs.

Normalize search terms and support aliases without creating separate manual search systems for every menu.

515. LIVE CONTENT — FEATURE FLAGS

Use server-controlled feature flags for new characters, weapons, maps, modes, events, UI experiments, and presentation changes.

Remove obsolete flags after rollout instead of accumulating dead code paths.

