525. IDEA MATRIX RULE

Combine category seeds only when the resulting system improves player choice, replayability, readability, world identity, performance, accessibility, or maintainability.
Do not implement every possible combination. Evaluate combinations for actual value.
When a useful combination is discovered, create a WIPModules layout if implementation will be staged later.
Keep all generated ideas original and avoid recognizable copies of protected game content.
The idea matrix is an expansion engine, not a stopping point.

526. WORKSPACE ASSET INTAKE — SOURCE SCANNER

Inspect the developer workspace for newly supplied open-source or otherwise authorized project assets before creating replacements.

Classify assets by animation, model, mesh, texture, decal, audio, VFX, UI, script, module, configuration, documentation, and test content.

Do not assume the source folder is organized correctly. Build an intake inventory before migration.

Preserve valid licensing or attribution metadata when required.



527. WORKSPACE ASSET INTAKE — REPLICATED STORAGE ORGANIZATION

Organize reusable shared assets into the established ReplicatedStorage framework rather than leaving the project dependent on random workspace folders.

Use clear categories for Characters, Weapons, Animations, VFX, Audio, UI, Maps, Shared, Config, Content, Remotes, and developer-only tooling where the existing architecture supports them.

Do not duplicate an asset merely because two systems use it; create a shared reference when appropriate.

Keep server-only assets out of client-visible locations when their visibility would be inappropriate.



528. WORKSPACE ASSET INTAKE — ANIMATION MIGRATION

Scan all supplied animations and match them to the appropriate character, weapon, movement, combat, cinematic, UI, or preview registry.

Remove duplicate references and identify missing animation dependencies.

Use animation markers consistently for important gameplay synchronization.

Do not assume an animation is valid merely because it loads once.



529. WORKSPACE ASSET INTAKE — MESH MIGRATION

Scan supplied meshes and classify them by world, character, weapon, UI preview, VFX, prop, or test usage.

Check geometry density, material usage, collision needs, LOD suitability, and reuse potential.

Do not ship temporary placeholder meshes when a valid source asset already exists.

Do not use unlicensed or extracted proprietary game assets.



530. WORKSPACE ASSET INTAKE — VFX MIGRATION

Collect supplied particle systems, beams, trails, attachments, effects, and VFX modules into a reusable VFX registry.

Convert repeated effects into data-driven effect definitions where practical.

Add owner, lifetime, quality tier, priority, pooling, and cleanup behavior.

Do not leave source VFX scattered across arbitrary folders.



531. WORKSPACE ASSET INTAKE — UI MIGRATION

Collect supplied UI assets and move them into the custom UI framework or an intentional asset library.

Standardize icons, borders, panels, textures, and preview assets through UI tokens and component definitions.

Repair scale, AnchorPoint, constraints, and aspect ratio during migration.

Do not preserve bad source layout merely because the source was previously working.



532. WORKSPACE ASSET INTAKE — SCRIPT MIGRATION

Inspect supplied scripts and modules for useful logic before moving them into production architecture.

Do not blindly merge source code into existing systems.

Extract reusable behavior, reconcile naming, validate dependencies, remove duplicate authority, and migrate only what is justified.

Retain useful comments from source layouts when they explain important behavior.



533. WORKSPACE ASSET INTAKE — CONTENT REGISTRY

Every migrated reusable asset should receive a stable content identifier where appropriate.

Register the asset exactly once.

Detect duplicates by asset identity, content ID, or semantic role where practical.

Record the migration path in the task ledger.



534. WORKSPACE ASSET INTAKE — AUTOMATIC ORGANIZER

Build a development-only organizer that can classify and propose destinations for workspace assets.

Use naming, tags, class types, folder context, and explicit configuration rather than guessing from file names alone.

Require validation before moving important content.

Never delete source content until the migrated result is confirmed and the original is safely preserved or intentionally archived.



535. REPLICATED STORAGE — SHARED CONTENT CONTRACT

Keep content that must be shared by client and server in appropriate shared locations.

Keep server-authoritative secrets and private logic server-side.

Keep client presentation assets accessible where they are intentionally needed.

Use the existing framework instead of inventing a second content root.



536. REPLICATED STORAGE — REMOTE ORGANIZATION

Centralize remotes under the project Remote architecture and keep naming consistent.

Do not create one-off RemoteEvents in arbitrary folders.

Document each remote contract and validate both sides.

Remove orphaned remotes after migrations.



537. REPLICATED STORAGE — WIPMODULES LOCATION

Keep WIPModules clearly separated from production modules.

WIP layouts may reference future production paths, but they must clearly declare those intended destinations.

Never allow production code to require WIP modules unless a deliberate development flag exists.



538. CONTENT PACK LOADER

Build a loader that can register large groups of characters, weapons, maps, animations, VFX, sounds, and UI assets from validated content packs.

Use deterministic registration order where ordering matters.

Protect each pack load with validation and failure reporting.

A failed optional pack must not prevent unrelated required packs from loading.



539. CONTENT PACK ROLLBACK

Support rolling back a bad development content pack without deleting unrelated systems.

Track pack version, dependencies, and affected registries.

Re-run validation after rollback.



540. CONTENT PACK DEPENDENCY GRAPH

Represent dependencies between packs such as a character needing animations, VFX, audio, UI, and ability definitions.

Detect cycles or missing dependencies before runtime activation.

Prefer explicit dependency declarations over hidden require chains.



541. CHARACTER SHOWCASE SYSTEM

Create a polished 3D showcase screen capable of presenting multiple original characters without loading full gameplay environments.

Support idle, combat preview, ultimate preview, outfit preview, weapon preview, and camera orbit.

Use isolated preview state so showcase animation cannot affect gameplay.



542. WEAPON SHOWCASE SYSTEM

Create an equivalent 3D weapon display with rotating presentation, animation, VFX preview, stats, mastery, and lore.

Keep preview rendering bounded and cleanly destroyed when the menu closes.



543. POWERUP SHOWCASE SYSTEM

Create a reusable preview presentation for powerups showing their animation, aura, icon, duration, and effects without executing the actual gameplay effect.



544. MAP SHOWCASE SYSTEM

Create a map selection presentation that can show a miniature or representative 3D environment, biome, landmark, mode compatibility, and estimated difficulty.

Use low-detail previews instead of loading an entire map just to display a thumbnail.



545. MATCHMAKING UX — QUEUE FEEDBACK

Display queue status, estimated state, party members, mode, map restrictions, and cancel controls.

Avoid fake timers or misleading estimated queue times.

Recover gracefully from server transitions.



546. MATCHMAKING UX — SEARCH RANGE

Where skill-based matchmaking is used, communicate a simple user-facing state such as searching, widening search, or match found without exposing sensitive hidden rating values.

Do not make players feel disconnected from the queue state.



547. DUEL UX — CHALLENGE PRESENTATION

Give duel requests a clear timeout, accept, reject, and context presentation.

Support quick rematch after results.

Do not allow stale challenge prompts to remain after a player leaves or changes mode.



548. PRIVATE LOBBY UX

Show lobby code, members, ready state, rules, map, and start conditions.

Make host-only controls obvious.

Use controller and touch navigation from the same input abstraction.



549. ROUND PRESENTATION

Create configurable round start, round end, match point, and final result presentation.

Use short, skippable transitions instead of long forced cinematics.



550. FINISHER FREQUENCY CONTROL

Create content-level controls for finisher presentation frequency.

Prevent every minor elimination from triggering maximum-length presentation.

Reserve high-cost camera and VFX sequences for intentionally important moments.



551. COMBAT TRAINING REPLAY

Allow training mode to record short input sequences and replay them against a dummy for testing combos.

Keep recording bounded by time and memory.

Make replay state deterministic enough to reproduce bugs where practical.



552. FRAME DATA DEV TOOL

Create a developer-only frame timeline for attack startup, active, recovery, cancel windows, hitstop, and resource changes.

Use actual configuration and animation markers where possible instead of manually typed labels.



553. HITBOX HEATMAP

Allow developers to visualize where repeated hits occur in an arena.

Use the tool to find oversized or inconsistent hitboxes and unfair safe zones.



554. COMBO ROUTE ANALYZER

Analyze which attacks can transition into which others and identify dead ends, infinite loops, impossible routes, or unusable timing windows.

Use this for design validation rather than player-facing automation.



555. STATUS SANDBOX

Create a developer sandbox where arbitrary combinations of statuses can be applied to test stacking, cleansing, conversion, immunity, resistance, and expiration.

All tests must be isolated from live persistence.



556. WEAPON SANDBOX

Allow developers to equip any test weapon, change mastery, alter stance, and spawn it in a safe training context.

Keep all test grants non-persistent.



557. POWERUP SANDBOX

Allow developers to spawn, stack, remove, expire, and inspect powerups in training mode.

Use the same live powerup code paths wherever possible.



558. MAP BUILDER — PROP FAMILY EXPANSION

Expand the procedural prop library with bins, benches, poles, cables, signs, barriers, planters, kiosks, crates, pallets, vents, rooftop machinery, utility boxes, street furniture, construction props, and many original decorative families.

Each family should support variants, budget, LOD, collision, and interaction policy.



559. MAP BUILDER — ARCHITECTURAL VARIATION

Generate varied facade shapes, window groupings, roof lines, entrances, color/material combinations, and setbacks.

Use composition rules so buildings feel related but not cloned.



560. MAP BUILDER — ROOFTOP ECOSYSTEM

Generate vents, water tanks, solar-like props, antennas, access doors, railings, signage, ledges, bridges, and parkour paths on rooftops.

Keep collision and navigation intentional.



561. MAP BUILDER — UNDERGROUND ECOSYSTEM

Generate tunnels, utility corridors, maintenance rooms, storage chambers, transit-like passages, drainage channels, and hidden arenas.

Keep routes navigable and avoid accidental soft locks.



562. MAP BUILDER — DESTRUCTION ECOSYSTEM

Generate breakable windows, signs, fences, crates, lamps, doors, lightweight walls, and decorative props with configurable destruction behavior.

Reserve expensive structural destruction for selected locations.



563. MAP BUILDER — LANDSCAPE MICRODETAIL

Generate pebbles, roots, fallen leaves, grass clumps, cracks, moss, puddles, weeds, and small debris using grouped decoration passes.

Cull or reduce these details aggressively at distance.



564. MAP BUILDER — WEATHER DECORATION

Allow rainfall puddles, damp surfaces, wind-driven foliage, storm debris, mist pockets, and weather-specific props.

Keep weather effects budgeted.



565. MAP BUILDER — EVENT ARENA CONVERSION

Allow a normal world region to temporarily become an event arena through registered event overlays.

Use temporary lighting, barriers, spawn anchors, objective markers, hazards, and destruction state.

Restore the underlying region cleanly after the event.



566. MAP BUILDER — MAP ROTATION

Assign generated and authored maps to weighted mode pools.

Support seasonal or event-specific availability without duplicating map data.



567. MAP BUILDER — MAP BAN / DISABLE

Allow administrators or developers to temporarily disable a broken map or generator version.

Do not erase the content; preserve it for debugging and repair.



568. MAP BUILDER — SEED FAVORITES

Allow developers to mark interesting procedural seeds as favorites for later polish and regression testing.

Keep seed records small and reproducible.



569. MAP BUILDER — SEED SEARCH

Support searching generated test results by seed, version, biome, quality score, and failure reason.



570. MAP BUILDER — BATCH GENERATION

Generate many candidate maps offline or in controlled development batches, then rank them using automated quality metrics.

Do not run an uncontrolled generator loop in live player servers.



571. MAP BUILDER — SNAPSHOT DIFF

Compare two map generations and identify changed chunks, props, landmarks, and budgets.

Use this to investigate why a generator version caused a regression.



572. WORLD EVENT — RARE SPAWN TELEGRAPH

Rare bosses and special encounters should have subtle but discoverable world telegraphing.

Examples include NPC rumors, distant VFX, audio changes, map marker escalation, or environmental anomalies.



573. WORLD EVENT — PLAYER DISCOVERY

Allow players to discover events organically through exploration, NPC dialogue, environmental cues, or visible world changes.

Do not force every event through a menu.



574. WORLD EVENT — EVENT CHAINS

Allow one resolved event to create a later consequence, follow-up encounter, reward opportunity, or new route.

Keep chain state authoritative and recoverable.



575. WORLD EVENT — EVENT FATIGUE CONTROL

Track event frequency per region so players are not repeatedly interrupted by the same type of encounter.

Rotate event families based on recent activity.



576. BOSS — UNIQUE ARENA RULES

Some bosses may interact with special arena rules such as destructible routes, vertical phases, moving cover, hazard timing, or temporary environmental states.

Use reusable arena modifier modules.



577. BOSS — LEARNING CURVE

Bosses should teach their mechanics before escalating them.

Telegraphs and recovery windows should become understandable through repetition rather than arbitrary surprise spam.



578. BOSS — MULTI-PLAYER SCALING

Scale boss behavior from player count using controlled formulas rather than simply multiplying health.

Consider target selection, attack frequency, adds, arena hazards, and rewards.



579. NPC — AMBIENT CONVERSATION

Use short ambient dialogue snippets to communicate world state and reinforce regional personality.

Do not run full dialogue UI for every ambient line.



580. NPC — CROWD DENSITY

Generate believable clusters of NPCs while avoiding excessive per-agent simulation.

Use group behaviors where individual detail is unnecessary.



581. NPC — PANIC PROPAGATION

In large events, nearby NPCs can share simplified panic state rather than each individually making expensive decisions.

Use region events and group state.



582. NPC — SHOPKEEPER STATES

Shopkeepers can react to closing time, world events, danger, quests, or player reputation.

Use shared schedule and reaction systems.



583. NPC — COMPANION TELEPORT RECOVERY

Companions that fall too far behind can use safe catch-up logic rather than breaking their navigation state.



584. DIALOGUE — VOICELESS PRESENTATION

Support dialogue that feels alive without requiring voice acting for every line.

Use timing, portraits, expressions, gestures, camera emphasis, UI motion, and short sound cues.



585. DIALOGUE — INTERRUPTIBLE LINES

Allow certain dialogue lines to be interrupted by world events, combat, or player movement while preserving state consistency.



586. DIALOGUE — MEMORY OF CHOICES

Store important prior choices as structured state and use them consistently in later conversations.

Do not make every dialogue branch depend on dozens of scattered boolean flags.



587. DIALOGUE — CHOICE TAGS

Tag choices by tone, intent, risk, empathy, aggression, curiosity, or other original categories when the story system benefits from it.

Use tags as data, not as an excuse for hidden morality math.



588. QUEST — PROCEDURAL OBJECTIVES

Build reusable objective generators for travel, interaction, combat, collection, parkour, protection, investigation, destruction, race, defense, and challenge tasks.

Validate generated objectives before exposing them to players.



589. QUEST — OPTIONAL OBJECTIVES

Use optional objectives to encourage mastery without forcing them into the critical path.

Make rewards proportional and readable.



590. QUEST — FAIL STATE DESIGN

Every timed, escort, defense, or protection quest must define what happens on failure and how the player can recover.



591. QUEST — REPEATABLE QUEST VARIANTS

Generate bounded variations of repeatable tasks so they do not feel identical every time.



592. PROGRESSION — MASTERY TRACKS

Create separate mastery tracks for characters, weapons, parkour, bosses, and game modes where they add value.

Avoid redundant XP bars for every trivial action.



593. PROGRESSION — MASTERY CHALLENGES

Use challenge-based mastery objectives such as successful counters, movement chains, status conversions, or environment interactions.

Reward expressive play rather than brute-force repetition.



594. PROGRESSION — COSMETIC REWARD PATHS

Prefer cosmetic or expressive rewards for long-term mastery when raw power would destabilize balance.



595. ECONOMY — SOURCE/SINK AUDIT

For every currency, identify sources and sinks.

Detect inflation risk, dead currencies, and rewards with no meaningful sink.



596. ECONOMY — PRICE VALIDATOR

Validate shop prices, reward quantities, upgrade costs, and transaction limits through centralized data definitions.



597. ECONOMY — REWARD DUPLICATE PROTECTION

Make reward transactions idempotent and tie them to authoritative sources such as match IDs, quest IDs, or event IDs where appropriate.



598. SHOP — PREVIEW WITHOUT OWNERSHIP

Allow players to preview unowned characters, weapons, outfits, and cosmetics without granting ownership.

Preview must be isolated from gameplay authority.



599. SHOP — FAVORITE AND HIDE

Allow favorite and optional hide controls for large shop catalogs.

Keep filtering client-side while purchase authority remains server-side.



600. COLLECTION — COMPLETION TRACKING

Show progress toward useful collections without requiring players to open dozens of separate menus.



601. COLLECTION — DISCOVERY HINTS

For hidden collections, provide optional hints after repeated exploration or through NPC dialogue.



602. SEASON — CONTENT ROTATION

Rotate maps, events, cosmetics, challenges, and optional modes without deleting permanent player ownership.



603. SEASON — END-OF-SEASON SAFETY

When a season ends, reconcile rewards, rank state, and unfinished challenges before resetting seasonal presentation.



604. SOCIAL — MATCH SPECTATING

Allow friends or eligible players to spectate compatible matches while respecting privacy and information policies.



605. SOCIAL — SPECTATOR DELAY

Where competitive integrity requires it, consider controlled spectator delay rather than real-time hidden-information exposure.



606. SOCIAL — REMATCH FLOW

Make rematches fast while ensuring old match state is fully cleaned before the new match begins.



607. SOCIAL — PARTY READY STATE

Party readiness should be visible and should survive harmless UI transitions without duplicating ready requests.



608. PHOTO MODE — POSE LIBRARY

Support original pose presets and character-specific poses without modifying live combat state.



609. PHOTO MODE — WORLD FREEZE

When photo mode freezes presentation, distinguish visual freeze from authoritative gameplay state and do not grant exploit-prone invulnerability in live competitive modes.



610. PHOTO MODE — UI CLEANUP

Hide and restore HUD layers cleanly when photo mode opens and closes.



611. REPLAY — EVENT COMPRESSION

Use compact event records for major actions rather than storing every rendered transform sample if the feature does not need it.



612. REPLAY — VERSION COMPATIBILITY

Store replay format versions and handle incompatible replays safely rather than crashing the viewer.



613. REPLAY — PERFORMANCE

Keep capture bounded by memory and server/client budget.



614. UI — SCREEN ROUTER

Create a shared screen router with stack, back, modal, focus, open, close, replace, and cleanup behavior.

Do not allow screens to invent their own navigation stack.



615. UI — COMPONENT STATES

Reusable UI components should have explicit states such as idle, focused, hovered, pressed, disabled, loading, error, selected, and locked where relevant.



616. UI — UI TRANSITION CANCELLATION

Interrupting a transition must stop or reconcile the old animation before starting the new state.

Never accumulate overlapping tweens or connections indefinitely.



617. UI — 3D WORLD MARKER PRIORITY

World markers should have priority classes so critical objectives, bosses, and hazards remain readable while cosmetic markers can be hidden.



618. UI — WORLD MARKER EDGE FALLBACK

Important off-screen markers may use screen-edge indicators when direct world-space positioning is not useful.



619. UI — ANCHOR AUTO-FIX TOOL

Build a developer utility that proposes safe AnchorPoint and Scale-based replacements for obviously broken centered layouts.

Do not apply bulk fixes blindly; validate the resulting composition.



620. UI — RESPONSIVE REGRESSION SNAPSHOTS

Maintain representative screenshots or structured layout measurements for important screens across multiple aspect ratios.

Use differences to detect regressions.



621. UI — INPUT PROMPT RESOLUTION

Resolve the correct keyboard, controller, or touch prompt from the shared input action ID.

Do not hard-code device-specific text inside gameplay modules.



622. UI — VIRTUALIZED COLLECTION PREFETCH

Virtualized lists may pre-create a small buffer around the visible range to reduce scrolling hitching.

Keep the buffer bounded.



623. UI — AUTOMATIC SIZE SAFETY

Audit AutomaticSize chains so parent and child sizes do not recursively depend on each other in ways that create unstable layout behavior.



624. UI — TEXT OVERFLOW

Handle unexpectedly long names, translated strings, item descriptions, and quest text without clipping critical information.



625. UI — DISPLAY SCALE PRESETS

Provide sensible default UI scale presets and a continuous or stepped custom scale where practical.



626. ANIMATION — STATE RECONCILIATION

When an animation is interrupted, restore the intended pose or let the next layer transition smoothly instead of leaving the rig in a broken transform state.



627. ANIMATION — SPEED MODIFIERS

Support safe animation speed changes for slow-motion, haste, cinematic, and training scenarios without breaking marker timing semantics.



628. ANIMATION — MARKER VALIDATION

Search for required animation markers referenced by gameplay definitions and flag missing markers before playtesting.



629. ANIMATION — FALLBACK CLIPS

Provide simple fallback clips for optional presentation when the preferred animation asset is unavailable.



630. ANIMATION — PROCEDURAL BLEND LIMITS

Keep procedural joint offsets bounded so they cannot destroy authored animation readability or create unstable poses.



631. VFX — EFFECT GRAPH

Compose complex effects from reusable child effects such as burst, ring, trail, beam, flash, dust, debris, and aura rather than one monolithic asset for every ability.



632. VFX — EFFECT BUDGET ESTIMATOR

Estimate the cost of a VFX definition from emitter count, particle rate, transparency layers, lights, trails, and expected concurrency.

Use this estimate during content validation.



633. VFX — DISTANCE FADE

Distant effects should fade or downgrade based on configured relevance rather than staying at maximum cost.



634. VFX — PRIORITY EVICTION

When effect budgets are exceeded, remove or downgrade the lowest-priority cosmetic effects first.



635. AUDIO — EVENT PRIORITY

Prioritize critical combat and UI sounds over distant ambient audio during overload.



636. AUDIO — DUPLICATE SUPPRESSION

Identical short sounds triggered excessively should use controlled suppression, pooling, or aggregation.



637. AUDIO — MUSIC INTERRUPT

When a higher-priority music state starts, fade or transition the previous state cleanly.



638. CAMERA — SHAKE NORMALIZATION

Normalize multiple simultaneous impulses so the total camera movement remains controllable.



639. CAMERA — CAMERA RESET

Provide an explicit reset path after respawn, death, teleport, cinematic completion, menu close, spectator exit, and character switch.



640. CAMERA — DEVICE AWARENESS

Adjust default sensitivity and UI-related camera interactions for mouse, controller, and touch while keeping user overrides available.



641. PHYSICS — CONSTRAINT OWNERSHIP

Every created constraint or joint modification must have a lifecycle owner and cleanup policy.



642. PHYSICS — RUNTIME PART CREATION

Prefer existing map geometry or pooled parts where possible instead of creating large numbers of transient physical objects for minor effects.



643. PHYSICS — PROJECTILE POOL

Pool high-frequency projectile presentation objects when profiling supports the optimization, and reset all state before reuse.



644. AI — PATH REQUEST THROTTLING

Throttle repeated path requests from the same agent when the destination has not meaningfully changed.



645. AI — TARGET CACHE

Cache target candidates briefly where valid rather than rescanning the entire world every frame.



646. AI — GROUP BEHAVIOR

Use shared group state for crowds and squads to reduce redundant individual decisions.



647. AI — FAILURE ISOLATION

A broken NPC behavior callback should not terminate the entire AI scheduler. Use protected boundaries, diagnostics, and bounded disable/retry logic.



648. SECURITY — RATE LIMIT DASHBOARD

Create a developer-only dashboard showing remote request rates by player and action category.

Use it to discover spikes and exploit attempts.



649. SECURITY — INVALID STATE REPORT

Record invalid gameplay state transitions with enough context to reproduce them, while keeping internal exploit-detection logic private from players.



650. SECURITY — SERVER RESULT AUDIT

Audit every path that changes a player-facing persistent value to ensure the result originates from an authoritative server decision.



651. QUALITY — BUG REPRODUCER

Create a generic reproduction record format that can capture feature ID, character, map, mode, action sequence, seed, configuration, and timing when a bug is discovered.



652. QUALITY — REGRESSION TAGGING

Tag tests by subsystem and severity so a shared framework change can trigger the relevant focused regressions.



653. QUALITY — FAILING FEATURE LOCK

Do not let a feature be marked REVERIFIED while any known reproducible failure remains inside its defined scope.



654. QUALITY — CROSS-DEVICE AUDIT

For major player-facing features, test at least one keyboard/mouse, one controller, and one touch configuration where applicable.



655. QUALITY — CROSS-QUALITY AUDIT

Test major VFX and UI features at both low and high quality profiles to catch logic hidden inside presentation code.



656. QUALITY — CROSS-RESPAWN AUDIT

Repeat major gameplay flows after respawn to detect leaked state, camera locks, stale connections, or duplicate UI.



657. QUALITY — CROSS-TELEPORT AUDIT

Test relevant systems across teleport, server change, match transition, or streamed region transitions where applicable.



658. QUALITY — LONG-SESSION AUDIT

Run extended sessions that repeatedly exercise normal loops to detect slow memory growth, task accumulation, connection leaks, and degraded performance.



659. QUALITY — IDLE SESSION AUDIT

Leave a server or menu state idle for a controlled interval and confirm expensive systems are throttled rather than consuming constant maximum resources.



660. QUALITY — RETURN-FROM-MENU AUDIT

Open and close major menus repeatedly and verify navigation, focus, animations, audio, and connections remain stable.



661. QUALITY — RAPID-ACTION AUDIT

Rapidly activate, cancel, and reactivate abilities, dodges, attacks, interactions, and UI actions to expose race conditions.



662. QUALITY — SHUTDOWN AUDIT

Exercise graceful shutdown handling for active matches, saves, map generation, world events, and scheduled tasks.



663. QUALITY — TOOL FAILURE AUDIT

When a development tool fails, record the failure and continue with unaffected work rather than ending the full implementation task.



664. QUALITY — FILE CHANGE RECONCILIATION

If a file changes underneath the agent, reread current content, reconcile intended changes, and continue instead of overwriting newer valid work blindly.



665. QUALITY — LEDGER EVIDENCE

Every REVERIFIED item should point to enough implementation and validation evidence that another agent can understand why it passed.



666. FINAL EXPANSION LOOP — REVISIT WIP

After major implementation passes, revisit WIPModules before declaring the project finished.

Implement valuable prepared layouts and update them as architecture evolves.



667. FINAL EXPANSION LOOP — REVISIT SOURCE ASSETS

Rescan the source workspace for newly available authorized assets and integrate useful additions through the asset-intake pipeline.



668. FINAL EXPANSION LOOP — REVISIT MAP GENERATOR

Generate additional candidate maps, inspect their quality scores, and promote strong results into actual playable content.



669. FINAL EXPANSION LOOP — REVISIT UI

Audit every actual screen against the scale, AnchorPoint, aspect ratio, safe-area, accessibility, and input standards.



670. FINAL EXPANSION LOOP — REVISIT PCALL

Search all callback boundaries, asynchronous jobs, content loaders, lifecycle functions, and remote handlers for appropriate pcall/xpcall protection and visible failure handling.



671. FINAL EXPANSION LOOP — REVISIT PERFORMANCE

Profile again after content volume has increased because an optimization pass made before the final content set is not automatically representative of final workload.



672. FINAL EXPANSION LOOP — REVISIT SECURITY

Re-audit newly added content and systems for client trust, authority, rate limiting, ownership, and reward integrity.



673. FINAL EXPANSION LOOP — REVISIT CLEANUP

Repeat creation/destruction tests for newly added content and systems.

New features must not inherit old assumptions about cleanup.



674. FINAL EXPANSION LOOP — REVISIT ORIGINALITY

Audit new characters, weapons, maps, UI, VFX, animations, names, and story content for accidental resemblance to protected source material.



675. FINAL TERMINATION RULE — STILL CONTINUE

No section number, milestone, content count, WIP backlog size, asset count, map count, character count, or successful test count authorizes termination.

Continue until the existing final completion gate and every newly discovered applicable requirement are reverified.


